Z||000017||LZeitgeist||10-14-2001||09:17 PM||||Here in this forum, as well as most other places on the Internet, a mental picture is formed in the mind of the reader about the person who wrote the message.<P>Every impression hinges on how the message is written. <P>Poor spelling, absent punctuation and unintelligible sentence structure paint a very poor picture of the writer, no matter how intelligent or well-spoken they happen to be in Real Life. Excuses like 'being in a hurry' don't excuse the picture painted because if the writer is unconcerned enough about their message that they choose to be imprecise and inaccurate, then why should the reader be forced to spend more time and effort deciphering the message than the writer was willing to put into it in the first place?<P>Like it or not, all of our words create the 'faces' we wear while we are online and give impressions about who we are and how we think. What sort of light do *you* want to cast on yourself when speaking to a truly global audience...?<P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - 2001 Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show||||reg||1
